<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-2-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>First-Order Linear ODEs</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Classify ODEs as linear or nonlinear using the definition. </p></li>
            <li><p> Solve first-order linear ODEs using integrating factors. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-linear-first-order">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall that a first-order ODE is <alert> linear </alert> if and only if it can be written in the form <md> a_1(x) \deriv{y}{x} + a_0(x)y = f(x) </md> When <m> f(x) \equiv 0 </m>, we say the equation is also <alert> homogeneous</alert>. Dividing the coefficient <m>a_1(x)</m> from both sides of the equation produces the <alert> standard form </alert> of the equation <md> \deriv{y}{x} + P(x) y = Q(x).</md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Let's first consider the homogeneous case, that is that <m>f(x) = Q(x) = 0</m>. In this case, the equation is separable. We can write 
        <md> 
            <mrow> \frac{dy}{y} \amp = -P(x) \, dx \implies </mrow>
            <mrow> y \amp = Ce^{-\int P(x)\, dx} </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Suppose now, though, that the equation is nonhomogeneous. Surely, the solution exponential we found above should still be a part of the "DNA" of the solution, as the <m>Py</m> term is still in the equation. But this time, we should expect a different particular solution. That is, we may expect a solution of the same form, but this time the <alert>parameter may vary</alert>. We suppose <md>y(x) = \mu(x) e^{-\int P(x) \, dx}.</md> Substituting this expression into the ODE gives us that <md> \deriv{\mu}{x} = Q(x) e^{\int P(x) \, dx}. </md> Therefore, we see the particular solution is <md> y_p(x) = e^{-\int P(x) \, dx} \int e^{\int P(x) \, dx} Q(x) \, dx.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Here's a clever trick to make this formula much easier. First, we compute <md> \mu(x) = e^{\int P(x) \, dx}. </md> If we multiply both sides of the general solution by <m>\mu</m>, then we get <md> \mu y = C + \int \mu(x) Q(x) \, dx. </md> But, applying the product rule, we can simply write this as <md> \deriv{d}{dx} [\mu y] = \mu Q. </md> How does this help?
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-solving-linear-first-order-ODEs">
        <statement>
            <p>
                We can follow the following steps to solve first-order, linear, ODEs. 
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Write the equation in standard form. 
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Compute the <alert> integrating factor </alert> <m> e^{\int P(x) \, dx} </m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Write the equation using the product rule.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Integrate both sides to find the general solution.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve <md> 3\deriv{y}{x} + 12y = 4 </md>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Following the steps from theorem <xref ref="thm-solving-linear-first-order-ODEs"/>, we write the equation as <md> \deriv{y}{x} + 4y = \frac{4}{3}. </md> Next, we compute the integrating factor <md> \mu(x) = e^{\int 4 \, dx} = e^{4x}. </md> Now, we can use the product rule trick to write the equation as <md> \deriv{}{x} [ e^{4x} y] = \frac{4}{3}e^{4x}. </md> Integrating both sides of the equation with respect to <m>x</m> gives us <md> e^{4x}y = \frac{1}{3}e^{4x} + C. </md> Therefore, the general solution is <md> y = \frac{1}{3} + Ce^{-4x}.</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</section>
